A||N||5||Polarbear||Engine transplant||||5||
Z||000000||Polarbear||08-29-2001||05:17 AM||||Hi there all Fiero fans!<P>I have a “sick” Fiero that requires an engine transplant. However, in my country there are rules as to what modifications are legal. Only a 20% increase in power is legal without a document from the carmaker stating what engine/Horsepower the car can handle. <P>Does anyone know if Pontiac has given out some guidelines as to what engines is suitable for the Fiero, and if so where to get hold of it?<P>What is the horsepower output of a stock V6 (87GT)?<P>I have my heart set on a 3800SC and a set of Porsche brakes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>87 Red GT (originally blue)<BR>I'm a system developer, not a mechanic!||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||08-29-2001||08:09 AM||||Hi and welcome to the forum!  What you do to your car will probably be decided by your government regulations and how deep your pocket is.  I suspect in Denmark any engine change is going to be expensive.<P>I think the stock v-6 runs about 140 HP.  The USA and European rating systems are not the same but a 3800SC will be way over a 20% increase.<P>If you decide to use a 3.4 iron head (Camaro) the Fiero externals bolt right on. Depending on the method the government uses to figure out what your motor is, you have an increase of 600 CC and it will appear exactly as the regular 2.8.<P>Is there a local engine/trans which could be modified to fit? May be cheaper. Have fun in your research.<P>Gary||||reg||12
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||04:06 PM||||welcome to the forum polar bear my question is this what kind of inspections do you have to go thru there and how would they know what motor you have in there ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||08-29-2001||05:12 PM||||Best I could say is maybe contact Cliff (owner/moderator of the Forum) to find out the name and info of the Fiero speciality shop in the Netherlands.  Since you are not a mechanic this would probably be a good place to start.  If all you are allowed is 20% then all that sounds like you could do is a 3.4  but since the 3.4 appears to the eye the same as the 2.8 maybe you could modify that past the 20% and no one would be the wiser. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||08-29-2001||06:33 PM||||You would probably have to track down some GM test of reasonablility (TOR) documents. I have no idea where you would be able to find them though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Polarbear||08-30-2001||10:37 AM||||Hi there all!<P>Thanks for the replies.<P>It is definitely some sort of "GM test of reasonablility" that I’m  looking for. There isn’t many GM cars sold here in Denmark, so I can’t turn to a dealer for the information. Right now the car is in Germany being repaired at DS-auto (www.ds-auto.de). The 2.8 have a lot of problems with sensors & oil-pressure. <P>As for what kind of inspections they do, I have no idea. I don’t expect it to be very thorough, but on the other hand I don’t want to be in a position where I have a perfectly running car, that are not legal to drive.<P>I know about the 3.4, which should have about 160 HP, and has it as an option.<BR>||||reg||1
